PASSWD(1)                      P32W Unix                       PASSWD(1)

NAME
       passwd - change a password

SYNOPSIS
       passwd [user]

DESCRIPTION
       passwd changes the password of user, or of the invoking user
       if none is given. Ordinary users may change only their own;
       they are first asked for the old one (Old password: ),
       unless the account has none. Root may change anyone's
       without the old one.

       The new password is asked for twice (New password:, Again: )
       with echo off. If the two differ, passwd says so and changes
       nothing. An empty new password removes the password: the
       account then logs in without one.

       The password is stored in the second field of /etc/passwd as
       a 21-character hash, ssss$hhhhhhhhhhhhhhhh: four hex digits
       of salt (chosen from the time and the process id) and a
       64-bit hash of salt and password, mixed 2000 rounds so that
       guessing is slow (passwd(5)). passwd writes the new file to
       /etc/ptmp, makes it mode 0644 and renames it onto
       /etc/passwd; the other lines are copied unchanged.

       passwd is set-user-id root (/bin/passwd, mode 4755), so that
       it can rewrite /etc/passwd.

EXAMPLES
       $ passwd
       Old password:
       New password:
       Again:
       Password changed.

       # passwd guest          (root: give guest a password)

FILES
       /etc/passwd    the accounts
       /etc/ptmp      the new file, while it is written

EXIT STATUS
       0 if the password was changed. 1 for an unknown user, a
       user who is not you (passwd: permission denied), a wrong
       old password (Sorry), new passwords that do not match, or a
       failure writing the file.

SEE ALSO
       login(1), su(1), adduser(8), passwd(5)

BUGS
       There is no lock: two passwd (or adduser) runs at once can
       lose one change. Any password is accepted, however short.
       Lines of /etc/passwd longer than 255 characters are broken.

       The hash is no defence for a stolen disk; it is meant only
       to keep passwords off the screen.
